WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.134 --> 00:00:02.102
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy-

00:00:02.102 --> 00:00:03.502
and Tony Award-winning actor

00:00:03.502 --> 00:00:06.105
you know from such shows as
"Breaking Bad" and "Your Honor,"

00:00:06.105 --> 00:00:09.175
along with films like "Argo,"
"Asteroid City," and "Trumbo."

00:00:09.175 --> 00:00:11.443
He stars in "Argyle,"
which is in theaters

00:00:11.443 --> 00:00:12.845
and IMAX on February 2nd.

00:00:12.845 --> 00:00:13.947
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:13.947 --> 00:00:15.648
our friend Bryan Cranston,
everybody!

00:00:15.648 --> 00:00:17.950
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:32.332 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:32.332 --> 00:00:34.266
You know, it's so lovely
to see you.

00:00:34.266 --> 00:00:36.269
-Thank you.
-We've talked in the past

00:00:36.269 --> 00:00:38.271
about your different facial hair
when you come.

00:00:38.271 --> 00:00:40.606
And can I just say something
that I mean sincerely?

00:00:40.606 --> 00:00:42.742
I feel like your clothes
always match

00:00:42.742 --> 00:00:45.278
whatever your current
facial hair is.

00:00:45.278 --> 00:00:47.213
-That's part
of the styling process, Seth.

00:00:47.213 --> 00:00:48.547
-Yeah.

00:00:48.547 --> 00:00:52.385
-Clearly you didn't
get that note. Yeah.

00:00:52.385 --> 00:00:54.120
-It doesn't matter
because it's immaterial to me

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.222
because I could never
grow, like, anything

00:00:56.222 --> 00:00:58.290
as luxurious as what
you're rocking right now.

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:00.326
-You can't? You can't
grow a mustache at all?

00:01:00.326 --> 00:01:02.896
-No.
-No, I see some, you know,

00:01:02.896 --> 00:01:04.330
5:00 shadow in there.
-You see something?

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:07.166
-Oh, well, no,
my makeup team puts that on.

00:01:07.166 --> 00:01:08.535
[ Laughter ]

00:01:08.535 --> 00:01:10.102
Gives me some masculine
confidence.

00:01:10.102 --> 00:01:12.806
-Street cred. There you go.
Yeah.

00:01:12.806 --> 00:01:14.740
It just kind of grows.
I don't know.

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:16.275
-Yeah. It's very nice.

00:01:16.275 --> 00:01:17.644
Everything about it,
it really is.

00:01:17.644 --> 00:01:19.112
-I don't know.
-I have some jealousy

00:01:19.112 --> 00:01:20.680
about the whole thing.
-Really?

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:22.515
-I do. Like, sometimes
you come here,

00:01:22.515 --> 00:01:24.016
you got a really good beard
sometimes.

00:01:24.016 --> 00:01:25.885
I mean, I thought it was
one of the nicest things about

00:01:25.885 --> 00:01:27.020
"Breaking Bad" is we saw, like,

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:28.888
a whole range
of different hair things.

00:01:28.888 --> 00:01:31.791
-Yeah, I had -- I had no hair.
I had hair.

00:01:31.791 --> 00:01:33.759
-A lot of people said it was
the writing and the acting.

00:01:33.759 --> 00:01:36.129
For me, it was all your hair.
-It was the hair.

00:01:36.129 --> 00:01:38.497
Or a lack thereof. Yeah.
-[ Laughs ] Or lack thereof.

00:01:38.497 --> 00:01:39.899
Hey, I want to lament.

00:01:39.899 --> 00:01:41.433
You are a Rams fan
I think in the same way

00:01:41.433 --> 00:01:42.935
that I am a Steelers fan,

00:01:42.935 --> 00:01:45.571
and I apologize that your season
also ended a little bit earlier

00:01:45.571 --> 00:01:47.039
than you would have liked.
-It was close.

00:01:47.039 --> 00:01:49.809
-It was close. It was
a great game against the Lions.

00:01:49.809 --> 00:01:51.944
-Yeah, yeah.
-And you actually filmed

00:01:51.944 --> 00:01:53.746
a video on the field.

00:01:53.746 --> 00:01:54.914
You were at the game.
-Right.

00:01:54.914 --> 00:01:57.083
-And, uh, first I want
to show the video

00:01:57.083 --> 00:01:58.784
because I guess
you were trying to,

00:01:58.784 --> 00:02:00.719
you know, have some confidence
about your Rams

00:02:00.719 --> 00:02:02.088
before the game.
Let's take a look.

00:02:02.088 --> 00:02:03.656
-Bryan Cranston here
on the sidelines

00:02:03.656 --> 00:02:06.125
of the Lions
versus the Rams.

00:02:06.125 --> 00:02:10.797
And just like in the wildlife,
when rams see lions out about

00:02:10.797 --> 00:02:13.733
they think, "You know,
they look friendly."

00:02:13.733 --> 00:02:15.434
Go Rams.
-Alright.

00:02:15.434 --> 00:02:16.936
-Yeah.
-You know what?

00:02:16.936 --> 00:02:18.804
You're just supporting your
team, and then sadly they lost.

00:02:18.804 --> 00:02:19.906
They gave it a good game.

00:02:19.906 --> 00:02:23.109
And then the Lions
social media team...

00:02:23.109 --> 00:02:24.710
well, I guess this
is called a troll right.

00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:26.980
They just posted a photo of you
and said, "I won."

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:30.182
[ Laughter ]

00:02:30.182 --> 00:02:33.352
-And now I can show a picture
of the 49ers and go,

00:02:33.352 --> 00:02:35.688
"No, you didn't."
-[ Laughs ] Oh, boy.

00:02:35.688 --> 00:02:37.624
How dare you?
That's a real Walter White.

00:02:37.624 --> 00:02:38.858
-That's a Walter White move.

00:02:38.858 --> 00:02:40.760
I'll come in there.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:42.928
-[ Laughs ]
-Solar plexus.

00:02:42.928 --> 00:02:45.164
-[ Stammering ]

00:02:45.164 --> 00:02:47.099
Well, like, my YouTube algorithm

00:02:47.099 --> 00:02:48.768
recommends "Breaking Bad" clips
to me.

00:02:48.768 --> 00:02:50.036
That's basically
all they recommend.

00:02:50.036 --> 00:02:51.303
-Really?
-I watched like three clips

00:02:51.303 --> 00:02:52.672
of "Breaking Bad" a week.

00:02:52.672 --> 00:02:54.907
Never gets old for me.
-Really?

00:02:54.907 --> 00:02:57.709
-It's just so good.
I know, I'm just...

00:02:57.709 --> 00:02:58.844
I know you're not here

00:02:58.844 --> 00:03:00.680
promoting a show you did
a long time ago...

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:02.682
-[ Laughs ] Yeah.
-...but it's just amazing

00:03:02.682 --> 00:03:05.751
to have made a show
that just holds up so well.

00:03:05.751 --> 00:03:08.888
-It's -- It was one of the
seminal moments of my life.

00:03:08.888 --> 00:03:11.791
I'm so proud of it,
and it changed my life.

00:03:11.791 --> 00:03:14.060
-Yeah.
-But I will say that

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:16.996
I've only seen every episode
once where --

00:03:16.996 --> 00:03:17.930
-Really?
-Yes.

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:19.565
And fans come up to me

00:03:19.565 --> 00:03:22.234
and they go, "Okay,
season three, episode seven,

00:03:22.234 --> 00:03:24.470
you did this and this and this,
and you said that.

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:25.805
What were you thinking
when this happened?"

00:03:25.805 --> 00:03:27.206
I go, "I said what?"

00:03:27.206 --> 00:03:29.676
You know, I don't really recall

00:03:29.676 --> 00:03:31.243
because it's been 11 years.

00:03:31.243 --> 00:03:34.914
Can you believe that? 11 years
since we were off the air.

00:03:34.914 --> 00:03:36.983
-Since you -- Oh, my God,
that's crazy.

00:03:36.983 --> 00:03:38.685
-Off the air. Yeah.
-Now, why --

00:03:38.685 --> 00:03:41.154
Are you someone who only watches
the things you do

00:03:41.154 --> 00:03:42.555
once all the time?

00:03:42.555 --> 00:03:43.656
-Yes.
-Or is that unique to -- Okay.

00:03:43.656 --> 00:03:45.891
-Yeah, I got to move on.
-Yeah.

00:03:45.891 --> 00:03:48.460
-Yeah, I don't spend
a lot of time in the past.

00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:51.598
-Yeah.
-A lot of time in the present --

00:03:51.598 --> 00:03:55.134
right here, right now, and
then with an eye on the future.

00:03:55.134 --> 00:03:57.570
-That's a very smart way to go
because every morning I wake up

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:59.838
and I make my whole family
watch an old "Weekend Update."

00:03:59.838 --> 00:04:01.840
-Is that right?
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:05.244
And they go,
"I want that Daddy back!"

00:04:05.244 --> 00:04:07.546
[ Laughs ]
-"Where he's got more hair!"

00:04:07.546 --> 00:04:10.149
-"He was the nice Daddy!"
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:10.149 --> 00:04:13.953
And I'm like, "He was nice
'cause he didn't have you yet!

00:04:13.953 --> 00:04:17.223
That's a man free of children!"
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:17.223 --> 00:04:19.191
-"Oh, he loves
telling jokes back then."

00:04:19.191 --> 00:04:20.492
-Yes.
-Um...

00:04:20.492 --> 00:04:23.896
this is such a fun film,
"Argylle."

00:04:23.896 --> 00:04:26.532
We're going to show a clip
in between acts here,

00:04:26.532 --> 00:04:27.966
but this is --

00:04:27.966 --> 00:04:30.870
Uh, it's from the people
who made "The King's Man."

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:33.906
-"King's Man."
-Such a fun spy story.

00:04:33.906 --> 00:04:36.041
I mean, you were in, you know,
"Argo," which is a film,

00:04:36.041 --> 00:04:37.543
you know, which takes, I think,

00:04:37.543 --> 00:04:38.811
like, espionage very seriously.
-Yeah.

00:04:38.811 --> 00:04:40.179
-This has a lot more fun
with it.

00:04:40.179 --> 00:04:42.982
Did you enjoy yourself?
-Oh, God, this is so much fun.

00:04:42.982 --> 00:04:47.319
Matthew Vaughn who directed
"The King's Man" movies...

00:04:47.319 --> 00:04:48.855
that it's so much fun.

00:04:48.855 --> 00:04:50.990
It's over-the-top violence,

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:55.327
which it carries on
into a point of ridiculousness

00:04:55.327 --> 00:04:58.564
and so much fun
that my character is actually,

00:04:58.564 --> 00:05:00.900
if you look carefully
at the scene,

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:04.970
you might see me actually
twisting the mustache, you know?

00:05:04.970 --> 00:05:07.640
Because I play
a nasty villain in this.

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:09.375
-Usually a director
would step in

00:05:09.375 --> 00:05:11.144
if an actor twisted
a mustache and say,

00:05:11.144 --> 00:05:12.611
"That's a little too much."

00:05:12.611 --> 00:05:15.514
-No, he's by the monitors
going, "I want more of that!

00:05:15.514 --> 00:05:20.620
More! More twisting!"
-Um, very, uh, incredible cast.

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:23.122
Uh, Sam Rockwell,
uh, Samuel L. Jackson,

00:05:23.122 --> 00:05:24.323
Bryce Dallas Howard.

00:05:24.323 --> 00:05:25.991
None of these people
you worked with before.

00:05:25.991 --> 00:05:28.527
-Well, no, I did work
with Sam Rockwell.

00:05:28.527 --> 00:05:30.329
-Okay.
-Uh, I did a movie called

00:05:30.329 --> 00:05:33.699
"The One and Only Ivan,"
which was based on a true story

00:05:33.699 --> 00:05:36.235
about a man who has a gorilla,

00:05:36.235 --> 00:05:38.303
and he doesn't know what to do
with him once he grows up.

00:05:38.303 --> 00:05:42.775
So he puts him in a,
like, a de facto circus,

00:05:42.775 --> 00:05:45.844
and he voiced
the gorilla of Ivan.

00:05:45.844 --> 00:05:47.513
-Gotcha.
-I would hear his voice

00:05:47.513 --> 00:05:49.215
all the time
while I was working

00:05:49.215 --> 00:05:51.451
in the live-action part of it,
yeah.

00:05:51.451 --> 00:05:53.452
But that was
the only person I worked with.

00:05:53.452 --> 00:05:55.421
And, quite frankly,
he could have done better.

00:05:55.421 --> 00:05:56.955
-Yeah.
-I think, so...

00:05:56.955 --> 00:05:58.191
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:05:58.191 --> 00:05:59.992
Well, I think it's --
I think everybody agrees

00:05:59.992 --> 00:06:02.194
that's the last time he's
voiced a gorilla in any movies.

00:06:02.194 --> 00:06:04.096
-He will never voice
another gorilla.

00:06:04.096 --> 00:06:06.698
-[ Laughs ]
-You know, Hollywood is vicious.

00:06:06.698 --> 00:06:08.234
-They're vicious.
-Oh!

00:06:08.234 --> 00:06:09.268
-You blow it once
with a gorilla voice...

00:06:09.268 --> 00:06:10.702
-Once? Out.
-Yep.

00:06:10.702 --> 00:06:12.205
Uh, I have more
to ask you about.

00:06:12.205 --> 00:06:14.374
We'll be right back with Bryan
after this.

